it's been a little while since I talked  about voter ID of course we've talked  about voter suppression and fraud around  elections many times including with the  great Brad Friedman from Brad blog and  we now have a new Rasmussen Reports poll  which finds that support for photo ID  laws is that 76% nearly exactly the  seventy-eight percent support registered  in 2006 when the latest movement to  scrap the laws kicked off 58 percent of  Democrats believe a photo ID must be  shown before voting and 92 percent of  Republicans and 78% of voters overall  not affiliated with either major party  support photo ID rules Dave Rubin from  the Rubin report is here to discuss  these findings with me yeah you know  whenever I see these numbers I am  immediately reminded that most people  don't realize that just getting a  so-called voter ID is not free and thus  it arguably is not constitutional yes so  there's so much here and I really love  this topic because voting the process of  voting and having your voice heard is  literally everyone uses the word  literally when they don't mean literally  but it is literally the most important  thing that you can do as a citizen right  we have awful I don't know offhand do  you happen to know what percentage of  people voted in our last presidential  election  I mean it's roughly a 50% right yeah  it's pretty pretty terrible I know that  Obama's first go-around in 2008 was  better but in general it's about 50% and  that's just terrible and then we all sit  here and complain so we have there's a  couple issues here one is people just  don't vote and that may be partly  because the voter ID laws but it's also  just because of general apathy people  feeling that the system is rigged  against them it's also because we vote  on a Tuesday which makes no sense to me  because a neither should be a weekend  when more people would be able to vote  or it should be a national holiday what  do you think about that Ackman that's  what I've been saying for a long time  that it's such an important day that we  have to we have to K if we don't have  enough days in the year we have to  cancel  mother holiday because we need Election  Day to be an actual holiday so people  can get out yeah I've tended to agree  with that although I've gotten some  pretty compelling arguments from my  audience when I talk about this saying  even if you made it a national holiday  if voting is not compulsory a lot of  people will just take the day off so  that that you that'll give you some  marginal increase in the number of  people voting but it's not going to be a  landslide so to speak you get some bump  that would actually not be negligible it  would be something because I'm sure  there are a big chunk of people that  just cannot you know if you work 9:00 to  5:00 you commute you don't get home  until 6:00 the polling thing closes at  7:00 like there it's an actual  logistical problem no question about it  and you know the other option which also  we're seeing more of is early voting  hours voting absentee and for the  possibility of electronic voting and  that would have a huge impact I think  because let's face it a lot of people  just don't want to do something that's  out of their routine that is less  convenient and when you combine that  with the reality as printed Sprint  Princeton studies have shown us that  when we think we have a democracy we do  maybe on paper but in reality it's the  legislative priorities of the rich that  are overwhelmingly more likely to be  made into policy you have people who say  I don't really feel like my vote matters  plus it's not that convenient so I'm not  going to vote yeah I mean I think the  system people view the system now is so  broken that they don't even see that why  voting would actually make a difference  so I'm getting back to the voter ID  portion of this do you think that there  should be some national card that is  free that every citizen gets so that you  have to show that when you vote because  I think that's a pretty simple way of at  least taking up some of the X factors  here in terms of fraud and all that  here's the thing it's simple in concept  because you say hey the card is free and  that's it if you don't have a card you  can't vote the problem is when you say  let's go to layer two right how do you  get the card what document do you need  in order to obtain that card and where  can you go to get that card this is  where when the analyses have been done  on so-called free voter ID we find out  well you've got to have either your  birth certificate or a passport right  those documents are not free if you in  order to get a copy of your birth  certificate you may have to get  something notarized if you don't have a  notary in your small town many people  literally now you have transportation  costs to get to where you can get it  notarized if it's truly going to be free  that's a conversation to have there's  sort of a misunderstanding as to what  free means right so so I get that for  sure but I do think you could make a  compelling argument that if all you  needed was a passport or you know  something look we all have some  documentation short of the few of us  that truly live off the grid pretty much  everyone you need an ID to buy a beer  most of us the huge percentage of us  have some sort of ID so I get that there  are some financial reasons why this  could be a problem but it seems to me  that just walking in and I know people  are gonna get angry at me for saying  this but just walking into a polling  station and saying I am Who I am  there is that doesn't feel right to me  either  but let me let me counter that with  something else which is let's assume for  a second that it were a huge problem we  had evidence that that's happening all  the time you could make a stronger  argument around just that argument but  it's almost non-existent right so I and  I totally I totally get that the only  reason I'm saying it is just antek  totally I remember one of the times when  I was living in New York City when I  went to vote and I didn't have to show  ID and that was the first time I  realized I was like wow I just walked in  here said I was you know Dave Rubin but  at my address but literally anyone could  have done it that just seemed odd to me  but I understand that there's no there's  no evidence that this kind of thing and  this kind of fraud is actually affecting  elections in any way and that's where I  start to get concerned when you see  those particularly on one side of the  political spectrum trying to quote fix a  problem that there's no evidence exists  that's a red flag and that's when when  you look at the data you realize Oh  higher voter turnout correlates with the  more progressive candidate winning that  really paints the picture pretty clearly  for me  right that says it right there and of  course the other portion of this that we  haven't even mentioned is that there's  so much gerrymandering going on where  they're literally they just rewrite  districts depending on who they want to  win that that may be a bigger problem at  least at the Congressional level then  figuring out voter ID at the national  level no question about it and that's a  whole other discussion let us know your  thoughts at least as far as voter ID is  concerned in the comments
 

